Here is the refined 15-day curriculum, mapped precisely to your specified tech stack (Python, PyTorch, LangChain, CrewAI, LangGraph, etc.).

Since you are covering everything from foundational Python and Deep Learning frameworks up to advanced multi-agent orchestrators, the course is structured to move rapidly from data foundations to fully autonomous architectures.

15-Day Tech-Stack Specific Curriculum (2 Hours / Day)
Phase 1: Foundations of AI Development (Days 1–3)
Day 1: Python for AI Development & Environment Setup

Setting up VS Code and Jupyter Notebooks for AI development.

Python essentials for data: Lists, dictionaries, list comprehensions, and JSON parsing.

Working with asynchronous Python (async/await)—critical for high-throughput LLM API calls.

Day 2: Deep Learning Foundations with PyTorch & TensorFlow

Core concepts of Tensors, computation graphs, and gradients.

Brief architectural overview of Neural Networks in PyTorch and TensorFlow.

Understanding Embeddings: How neural networks represent text mathematically.

Day 3: LLM APIs & Advanced Prompt Engineering

Connecting to OpenAI/Anthropic APIs via Python.

Structured Outputs: Forcing LLMs to return strict, parseable JSON data.

Advanced prompting mechanics: Chain-of-Thought (CoT) and ReAct framework design.

Phase 2: RAG & Vector Databases (Days 4–7)
Day 4: Building Data Pipelines with LangChain & LlamaIndex

The core RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipeline architecture.

Document Ingestion: Loading, splitting, and chunking documents using LangChain and LlamaIndex.

Generating and managing vector text embeddings using PyTorch-backed local models.

Day 5: Local vs. Cloud Vector Databases (FAISS, Pinecone, Weaviate)

Inside a Vector DB: Similarity metrics (Cosine, Dot Product) and Indexing (HNSW, IVF).

FAISS: Building rapid local vector indexes for prototyping.

Pinecone & Weaviate: Setting up cloud vector databases, metadata filtering, and hybrid search.

Day 6: Advanced RAG Strategies (Query Transformation & Reranking)

Overcoming naive retrieval limits (Lost in the middle, semantic disconnect).

Implementing Query Rewriting, Sub-Query Decomposition, and Sentence-Window Retrieval.

Using Cross-Encoder Rerankers to optimize the final top-K context.

Day 7: Semantic Kernel Foundations & RAG Evaluation

Introduction to Microsoft's Semantic Kernel: Plugins, Planners, and Native functions.

Bridging enterprise C# / Python stacks using Semantic Kernel.

Evaluating RAG systems (Faithfulness, Context Recall) using evaluation loops.

Phase 3: Single-Agent Systems & Tool Integration (Days 8–10)
Day 8: Introduction to AI Agents & Tool Calling

The Agentic Paradigm shift: From static pipelines to autonomous execution loops.

Native function and tool calling: Enabling LLMs to execute Python code, read local databases, or fetch web data.

Day 9: State Management with LangGraph (Single-Agent)

Introduction to LangGraph: Concepts of Graphs, Nodes, Edges, and State.

Building a deterministic, cyclic agent workflow (e.g., Try Action → Catch Error → Fix Protocol).

Persistence and Checkpointing: How to pause, save, and resume agent sessions.

Day 10: Task-Oriented Agents with Semantic Kernel

Leveraging Semantic Kernel’s native plugins for deterministic agent flows.

Creating automated workflows where the LLM selects its own tools dynamically via Semantic Kernel Planners.

Phase 4: Multi-Agent Systems & Agentic Frameworks (Days 11–13)
Day 11: Multi-Agent Choreography with CrewAI

Introduction to CrewAI: Tasks, Agents, Crews, and Process types.

Defining distinct Agent personas (Role, Goal, Backstory) and assigning them specialized tools.

Hands-on: Building a collaborative crew (e.g., Research Agent passes data to Copywriter Agent).

Day 12: Event-Driven Multi-Agent Conversational Flows with AutoGen

Introduction to Microsoft's AutoGen: Conversable Agents and event-driven architectures.

Setting up complex, multi-turn conversations between autonomous AI agents.

Human-in-the-Loop (HITL): Intercepting agent conversations for manual human oversight or approvals.

Day 13: Advanced Multi-Agent Graphs with LangGraph

Designing custom Multi-Agent architectures from scratch using LangGraph.

Supervisor-Worker patterns vs. Peer-to-Peer agent communication.

Managing shared global state across different agents.

Phase 5: Production, Guardrails & Capstone (Days 14–15)
Day 14: Agentic Security, Monitoring & Tracing

Handling security exploits: Prompt injections and data leakage in agent tools.

Observability: Tracing agent loops, API tokens, and reasoning chains using monitoring dashboards (e.g., LangSmith or Arize Phoenix).

Day 15: Capstone Project Deployment & Showcase

Building a user interface for agents using Streamlit or Chainlit.

Student project showcase (e.g., An autonomous market research crew built with CrewAI, or a localized RAG system built with Weaviate and LangGraph).

Daily 2-Hour Delivery Breakdown
To ensure students retain this heavy engineering content, structure each 2-hour block as follows:

First 50 Mins: Concepts & Architecture (e.g., Drawing the LangGraph state flow or Vector DB indexing strategy).

10 Mins: Interactive Q&A / Quick Break.

Remaining 60 Mins: Live Code-Along (Providing pre-configured Jupyter Notebooks via VS Code so students don't get bogged down by package installation errors).